 ## Context
 Example: `slugify('Hello World Again', max_length=12)` is `'hello-world'`, and `slugify('Supercalifragilistic word', max_length=5)` is `'super'`.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+- `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0.
+- `python3 -c "from strkit import truncate_at_boundary, __all__; assert 'truncate_at_boundary' in __all__"` exits 0 (helper lives in `strkit/words.py`, exported from `strkit/__init__.py`).
+- `python3 -c "from strkit import slugify as s; assert s('Hello World Again', max_length=12) == 'hello-world' and s('Supercalifragilistic word', max_length=5) == 'super'"` exits 0.
+- `truncate_at_boundary(slug, max_length, sep)` returns `slug` unchanged when `len(slug) <= max_length`; otherwise the longest prefix of whole `sep`-separated words of length at most `max_length` with no trailing `sep`; if the first word alone is longer than `max_length`, the first `max_length` characters of `slug`.
+- `truncate_at_boundary` with `max_length` below 1 raises `ValueError`, including when `slug` is empty.
+- `slugify(text, sep='-', max_length=None)`: `None` keeps the old behavior; any other value applies `truncate_at_boundary` with the same `sep`, so `slugify('', max_length=0)` raises `ValueError`.
+- `tests/test_slug.py` covers: both Context examples, `max_length=None`, an exact fit, a cut one character short of a boundary, a non-default `sep`, no trailing `sep`, and `ValueError` below 1 (including empty text).
+- `README.md` documents `max_length` under `slugify` and has a `truncate_at_boundary` section.
+- (guard) `git diff --stat` touches only `strkit/__init__.py`, `strkit/words.py`, `strkit/slug.py`, `tests/test_slug.py`, `README.md`, and `.sdlc/slug-max-length/`; `split_words` and the rest of `slugify` are otherwise unchanged.
