 ## slugify

-`slugify(text, sep='-')` lowercases the words of `text` and joins them with `sep`.
+`slugify(text, sep='-', max_length=None)` lowercases the words of `text` and joins them with `sep`.

 ```python
 >>> from strkit import slugify
@@ -12,6 +12,25 @@ Small string helpers.
 'hello-world'
 ```

+`max_length` caps the length of the slug. The slug is cut at a word boundary, so it is never longer than `max_length` and never ends with `sep`. If the first word alone is longer than `max_length`, the slug is cut to its first `max_length` characters. `max_length=None` (the default) leaves the slug as is. A `max_length` below 1 raises `ValueError`, even for empty `text`.
+
+```python
+>>> slugify('Hello World Again', max_length=12)
+'hello-world'
+>>> slugify('Supercalifragilistic word', max_length=5)
+'super'
+```
+
+## truncate_at_boundary
+
+`truncate_at_boundary(slug, max_length, sep)` is the helper `slugify` uses for `max_length`. It returns `slug` unchanged if it fits in `max_length`. Otherwise it returns the longest prefix of whole `sep`-separated words that fits, with no trailing `sep`. If the first word alone is too long, it returns the first `max_length` characters of `slug`. A `max_length` below 1 raises `ValueError`.
+
+```python
+>>> from strkit import truncate_at_boundary
+>>> truncate_at_boundary('hello-world-again', 12, '-')
+'hello-world'
+```
+
 ## split_words

 `split_words(text)` returns the alphanumeric words of `text`.
